data: {"symbol": "BAJFINANCE", "name": "Bajaj Finance Ltd", "basis": "consolidated", "built_at": "2026-11-09T21:17:12.283739+05:30", "sources": [{"id": "T1", "kind": "transcript", "title": "Q2 FY27 earnings call (Oct 2026)", "quarter": "Q2 FY27", "url": "https://www.bseindia.com/stockinfo/AnnPdfOpen.aspx?Pname=7873abcb-7f97-487d-a474-028b0a26feea.pdf"}, {"id": "T2", "kind": "transcript", "title": "Q1 FY27 earnings call (Aug 2026)", "quarter": "Q1 FY27", "url": "https://www.bseindia.com/stockinfo/AnnPdfOpen.aspx?Pname=b101e24e-b807-4c74-9025-90009f4b47a5.pdf"}, {"id": "T3", "kind": "transcript", "title": "Q4 FY26 earnings call (May 2026)", "quarter": "Q4 FY26", "url": "https://www.bseindia.com/stockinfo/AnnPdfOpen.aspx?Pname=a448b6b0-edb5-4b99-a122-d602950310af.pdf"}, {"id": "T4", "kind": "transcript", "title": "Q4 FY26 earnings call (Apr 2026)", "quarter": "Q4 FY26", "url": "https://cms-assets.bajajfinserv.in/is/content/bajajfinance/bajaj-finance-q4-fy26-earnings-conference-call-transcript?scl=1&fmt=pdf"}, {"id": "AR", "kind": "annual_report", "title": "Annual Report 2026", "quarter": null, "url": "https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/a2357f0b-2828-4790-8a02-61064bd1929b.pdf"}], "ledger": {"rows": [{"promise": {"id": "T3-1", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 4, "quote": "We expect that it should come back to double -digit growth or the company growth momentum between Q2 and Q3 of FY'27."}, "topic": "MSME growth", "kind": "qualitative", "metric": null, "target_low": null, "target_high": null, "unit": null, "horizon": "Q2 to Q3 of FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Target not yet due \u2014 tracking is not delivery"}, {"promise": {"id": "T3-2", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 7, "quote": "In terms of customer franchise, we continue to be confident of adding 15 - 17 million new customers in FY27."}, "topic": "customer franchise growth", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 15.0, "target_high": 17.0, "unit": "usd_mn", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Target not yet due \u2014 tracking is not delivery"}, {"promise": {"id": "T3-3", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 7, "quote": "In terms of AUM, we're confident of a 20% to 24% AUM growth, aided by new businesses that we've launched in the last few years as they begin to scale."}, "topic": "AUM growth", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "revenue_growth", "target_low": 20.0, "target_high": 24.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No verifiable later evidence"}, {"promise": {"id": "T3-4", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 7, "quote": "On opex to NTI, we expect to see continued improvement of 25 to 40 basis points from current levels."}, "topic": "opex to NTI", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 25.0, "target_high": 40.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Target not yet due \u2014 tracking is not delivery"}, {"promise": {"id": "T3-5", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 8, "quote": "We continue to guide for 4.4% to 4.6%."}, "topic": "ROA guidance", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 4.4, "target_high": 4.6, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Target not yet due \u2014 tracking is not delivery"}, {"promise": {"id": "T3-6", "citation": {"source_id": "T3", "page": 8, "quote": "Return on equity came in at 20% in the current quarter, we continue to guide for 19% to 20%."}, "topic": "ROE guidance", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 19.0, "target_high": 20.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Target not yet due \u2014 tracking is not delivery"}, {"promise": {"id": "T4-1", "citation": {"source_id": "T4", "page": 6, "quote": "In terms of customer franchise, we continue to be confident of adding 15 - 17 million new customers in FY27."}, "topic": "customer acquisition", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 15.0, "target_high": 17.0, "unit": "usd_mn", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T4-2", "citation": {"source_id": "T4", "page": 6, "quote": "In terms of AUM, we're confident of a 20% to 24% AUM growth, aided by new businesses that we've launched in the last few years as they begin to scale."}, "topic": "AUM growth", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "revenue_growth", "target_low": 20.0, "target_high": 24.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No verifiable later evidence"}, {"promise": {"id": "T4-3", "citation": {"source_id": "T4", "page": 6, "quote": "On opex to NTI, we expect to see continued improvement of 25 to 40 basis points from current levels."}, "topic": "opex to NTI", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 25.0, "target_high": 40.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T4-4", "citation": {"source_id": "T4", "page": 7, "quote": "Return on equity came in at 20% in the current quarter, we continue to guide for 19% to 20%."}, "topic": "return on equity", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 19.0, "target_high": 20.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q4 FY26"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T2-1", "citation": {"source_id": "T2", "page": 4, "quote": "Overall, we are confident of delivering 25 to 40 basis points improvement for FY '27 in opex to NTI."}, "topic": "opex to NTI improvement", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 25.0, "target_high": 40.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q1 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T2-2", "citation": {"source_id": "T2", "page": 6, "quote": "I am targeting that next year we should be delivering INR100,000 crores volume between customer centricity, AI and tech transformation and digital transformation, all these three as they come together, that's really what we are aiming to do so that from a push business, we rapidly transition towards being a full business."}, "topic": "digital platform volume target", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 100000.0, "target_high": 100000.0, "unit": "cr", "horizon": "next year", "made_in": "Q1 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T2-3", "citation": {"source_id": "T2", "page": 6, "quote": "On disbursements, AI, voice and text bots did INR2,500 crores, we will do INR11,000 crores INR12,000 in the full year this year."}, "topic": "AI disbursements", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 11000.0, "target_high": 12000.0, "unit": "cr", "horizon": "full year this year", "made_in": "Q1 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T2-4", "citation": {"source_id": "T2", "page": 6, "quote": "That should help us for our B2B business generate 0.5 million customers that we could not do earlier."}, "topic": "B2B customer generation via custom AI model", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 0.5, "target_high": 0.5, "unit": "cr", "horizon": null, "made_in": "Q1 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "No later evidence of the outcome yet"}, {"promise": {"id": "T1-1", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 4, "quote": "We are well on track to deliver between 60 million to 62 million new loans in FY27."}, "topic": "new loans", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 60.0, "target_high": 62.0, "unit": null, "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q2 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Too recent to judge"}, {"promise": {"id": "T1-2", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 5, "quote": "We are well on track to deliver 180-odd agentic applications and 600-odd agents for the year."}, "topic": "technology transformation", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 180.0, "target_high": 600.0, "unit": null, "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q2 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Too recent to judge"}, {"promise": {"id": "T1-3", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 12, "quote": "I would say probably between 26%, 27% growth we should deliver now on a full year basis."}, "topic": "loan growth", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "revenue_growth", "target_low": 26.0, "target_high": 27.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q2 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Too recent to judge"}, {"promise": {"id": "T1-4", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 12, "quote": "So that's the second point. On NIM, clearly, at this point in time, we are baking in two more increases in the current year that would take us to 6% kind of rates."}, "topic": "interest rates", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "other", "target_low": 6.0, "target_high": 6.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "FY27", "made_in": "Q2 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Too recent to judge"}, {"promise": {"id": "T1-5", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 15, "quote": "I'm not changing, I must say to you, the longer -term growth guidance of 23% to 25%."}, "topic": "loan growth", "kind": "quantitative", "metric": "revenue_growth", "target_low": 23.0, "target_high": 25.0, "unit": "%", "horizon": "longer term", "made_in": "Q2 FY27"}, "status": "pending", "method": "judged", "actual": null, "evidence": null, "rationale": "Too recent to judge"}], "counts": {"kept": 0, "partly": 0, "missed": 0, "pending": 19, "dropped": 0}, "credibility_pct": null, "note": null}, "snapshot": {"summary": [{"text": "Urban consumer finance grew by 43%, rural consumer finance by 63%, and MFI grew on a low base, grew 54%.", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 3, "quote": "urban consumer finance grew by 43%, rural consumer finance by 63%. MFI grew on a low base, grew 54%"}}, {"text": "Gold loan book portfolio continued to grow, it's now 4.1% of balance sheet.", "citation": {"source_id": "T1", "page": 4, "quote": "Gold loan book portfolio continued to grow, it's now 4.1% of balance sheet."}}]}, "trajectory": {"years": ["Mar 2022", "Mar 2023", "Mar 2024", "Mar 2025", "Mar 2026"], "revenue": [31633.0, 41411.0, 54972.0, 68832.0, 81985.0], "operating_profit": [9905.0, 16018.0, 19987.0, 22963.0, 27088.0], "margin_pct": [31.312237220624034, 38.68054381686025, 36.35850978680055, 33.36093677359367, 33.04019027870952], "pat": [7028.0, 11508.0, 14451.0, 16779.0, 19332.0], "revenue_cagr_pct": 26.88155862327344, "pat_cagr_pct": 28.783806766347997, "margin_change_bps": 173, "read": "growing", "lender": true}, "health": {"as_of": "Mar 2026", "debt_to_equity": null, "cfo_to_pat_3y": null, "fcf_3y_cr": null, "working_capital_days": null, "wc_days_change_3y": null, "roce_pct": null, "flags": [{"level": "watch", "text": "Lender: leverage and working capital are judged on capital adequacy and asset quality, which this view does not cover"}]}, "bull": [{"text": "The company achieved a strong revenue CAGR of 26.9% from March 2022 to March 2026.", "citation": {"source_id": "FACTS", "page": 0, "quote": "Revenue CAGR (Mar 2022\u2013Mar 2026): 26.9%"}}, {"text": "Net profit also registered a robust CAGR of 28.8% over the same period.", "citation": {"source_id": "FACTS", "page": 0, "quote": "Net profit CAGR: 28.8%"}}, {"text": "The financing margin expanded with a change of plus 173 basis points.", "citation": {"source_id": "FACTS", "page": 0, "quote": "Financing margin change: +173 bps"}}], "bear": [{"text": "Out of 19 total recorded promises in the ledger, none have been kept or resolved so far.", "citation": {"source_id": "FACTS", "page": 0, "quote": "Promise ledger: 0 kept, 0 partly, 0 missed, 19 pending, 0 dropped (0 of 19 promises resolved)"}}], "notes": [], "cost": {"calls": 9, "cached": 9, "input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "usd_list_price": 0.0}, "build_seconds": 0.3, "guard": {"promises_proposed": 26, "promises_kept": 19, "points_proposed": 6, "points_kept": 6, "verdicts_downgraded": {"other_period": 0, "restated": 0, "below_target": 0}}, "excerpts": {"T3": {"4": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  April 29, 2026 \n \nPage 3 of 18 \n \n The principal objective of this panel is to provide a simplified reconciliation of core \nfinancial performance with that of reported numbers. That's the objective. Let me \nstart, first of all, with Q4 FY'25 last year. In Q4 last year, the company had recorded \nan additional ECL provision of INR  359 crores. Also in Q4 FY '25, the company had \nrecorded a tax benefit of INR 348 crores. That's Q4 last year. \n In Q4 this year, the company has taken an additional ECL provision of INR  142 \ncrores towards management and macroeconomic overlay. That's point number \none. Two, the company has also revised disclosure of recoveries against written -\noff loans. This was earlier presented under other operating income. It will \nhenceforth effective Q4 FY'26 will be reported under loan losses and provisions as \na net number. This has no impact on PBT and PAT for the quarter, but changes the \nratios. It principally leads to redefining 2 key metrics, which are important for \ninvestors and for us as management, which is opex to NTI and loan loss to average \nAR. \n As a result of this change, the opex to NTI is increasing from 33.2%, which is what \nit has come in to 33.8% as a result of this reporting change. And loan loss to average \nAR is reducing from 1.75% to 1.65% , this is really how we will report going forward \nas a company. The analysts will have to adjust the numbers to reflect this. \n I will summarize once again, this panel principally is intended to help clearly depict \nthe core profitability, which has seen a growth of 26% on PBT, as you can see, and \n27% for PAT.  I'll quickly cover the key points on Panel 6, 7 and 8. I'll only cover the \nhigh points. As I already said, AUM crossed INR 5 lakh crores. The growth was pretty \nsecular across all businesses. The gold loan portfolio continued to witness strong \nmomentum. It grew by 115%. The business now contributes to 3.5% overall AUM. \nWe foresee gol d loan portfolio to probably cross 5 -odd percent of total AUM by \nFY'27. \n Other lines of businesses also continue to grow in a healthy manner. That's covered \nin Panel 67 later in the slides. MSME continue to see muted growth. It grew by 6% \nonly on account of a set of proactive risk actions that we have been taking since \nQ2 FY '26. We expect that it should come back to double -digit growth or the \ncompany growth momentum between Q2 and Q3 of FY'27. Cost of funds improved \nby 4 basis points in Q4 and came in at 7.41%. NIM remained steady. \n Deposit book came in at INR  68,533 crores. It contributed to 16% of consolidated \nborrowings. Opex to NTI, going back to the earlier point that I made, came in at \n33.8% in Q4 as against 33.6%. These are new numbers. Otherwise, it came in at \n33.2% an improvement of 10 basis points versus Q4 last year. There was a marginal \nincrease sequentially, primarily due to,  I'm talking sequential on account of cost of \nnew Labour Code and accelerated gold loan branch expansion. ", "7": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  April 29, 2026 \n \nPage 6 of 18 \n \nI mean, while the West Asia conflict is going on one hand, when we look at the \nconsumer momentum on the ground, it's very likely we'll cross 5 million-plus loans \nin the month. Our last peak was 4.6 -odd million loans, and that was in festival \nperiod. So clearly, the company continues to generate momentum. \n Overall, last year, we booked 52.5 million loans and added 17.5 million new \ncustomers on a full year basis. AUM, I talked about grew 22%. PAT grew 24%, ROA , \nand opex to NTI I talked about it, showed an improvement of 36 basis points despite \nrapid expansion of gold loan branches, reasonable expansion of MFI branches by \nthe firm last year.  \nSo that's really on the full year performance. You guys anyway track it, so I don't \nhave to cover that. Let me cover what is important as to how do we see the outlook \nto be. Just on the previous year gone by , the Board of Directors have \nrecommended a dividend to shareholders of INR 6 per equity share.  \nSo that's a 600% dividend. It's broadly in line with the dividend last year as we had \noutlined that part of the dilution that we do in BHFL will be shared with the \nshareholders. So, we've in that spirit, continue to stay on course of that as well. So \nthat's really last year. Let me now go to important panel as to how do we see the \nmanagement assessment to be from a next fiscal standpoint.  \nAs I said, the year so far seems to have started pretty well. We are well on course \nto cross 5 million loans in a single month as a firm. I'm on Panel 28. Of course, I must \njust flag that the FY27 assessment is based on expectations of easing geopolitical \ntensions and macro stability. That  -- I'm sure , it's understood, but I thought it's \nimportant I make the point yet again that it's contingent upon easing geopolitical \ntensions and macro stability. \n Subject to that, the first point is on transformation making the business AI ready. I \nhave already talked about it. We will see significant acceleration and deployment \nof AI use cases across revenue, cost, customer engagement, underwriting, \ncontrollership, security as a firm. In terms of customer franchise, we continue to \nbe confident of adding 15 - 17 million new customers in FY27.  \nIn terms of AUM, we're confident of a 20% to 24% AUM growth, aided by new \nbusinesses that we've launched in the last few years as they begin to scale. Even \nlast year, they contributed 3% of the total growth. Rather 3.5% of total growth even \nin the previous fiscal. And as we gain greater confidence, our market share in those \nbusinesses and their contribution to the business should grow.  \nNIM, we expect marginal moderation. It's a little bit contingent on how interest \nrates play out, caused by geopolitical tensions. On opex to NTI, we expect to see \ncontinued improvement of 25 to 40 basis points from current levels. On credit cost, ", "8": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  April 29, 2026 \n \nPage 7 of 18 \n \nthis is a new credit cost metric, which, as I said we just reported a 165 basis points \nor 1.65% credit cost to AUM, the revised metric.  \nWe expect this to continue to trend down. The reasons I talked about earlier, which \nis winding down of 2-wheeler portfolio and in the MSME business being out of the \nwoods by June'26. In terms of profitability, we are pretty optimistic about the profit \ngrowth. ROA came in as of fourth quarter at 4.6%. We continue to guide for 4.4% to \n4.6%. \n Return on equity came in at 20% in the current quarter, we continue to guide for \n19% to 20%. And GNPA and NNPA, we expect them to remain range bound and well \nwithin our long-term guidance. I think that's really all I have to cover. Just quickly \non BHFL, company has already reported its performance.  \nThey had a good quarter on disbursements, AUM, asset quality, opex and profit, \ntheir disbursements and AUM growth remained strong at 23%. Their loan approvals \nare up a record 37%, opex to NTI continued to improve for them, came in at 19.2% \nas against 21.8% . I'm sure Atul has shared what his long -term view on where he \nforesees opex to NTI to get to, to be a pre -eminent mortgage company in this \ncountry. \n PBT for them in Q4 grew by 20% and PAT excluding onetime reversals, grew by \n20% and asset quality continue to remain very, very healthy with GNPA and NNPA \nat 27 and 11 basis point s respectively. BFSL though small, continue s to grow, \ndelivered a 77% growth in AUM and their profits were up 50%.  \nWe added 124,000 new customers in Q4 and delivered an ROE of 10.5%. So that's \nreally on BFL Q4 and its subsidiaries, the management guidance for FY27. Just the \nlast point, which I did not cover, I should have covered in terms of portfolio quality \nacross Panel 70 onwards,  continue to all be in green are seeing sequential \nimprovements across all portfolios.  \nOnly business loan and professional loans is yellow, which I've articulated twice \nover the last 20 minutes on how we foresee that this should turn. This will not turn \ngreen in a quarter because the denominator also has a role to play as it starts to \ngrow by Q2 or Q3, we will start to see this turn to green. That's really the quarter \ngone by, year gone by and our guidance or management assessment for next year. \nWe are happy to take questions with management. \nModerator: Thank you very much. We will now begin with the question and answer session. The \nfirst question is from the line of Abhishek Murarka from HSBC. Please go ahead. \nAbhishek Murarka: Hi, Rajeev, hi Sandeep. Thanks for taking my question. So just to get this whole \nguidance of credit costs very clear, if you don't mind, can I refer to the press release \nwhere you've given a summary of consolidated financial results? "}, "T4": {"6": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  April 29, 2026 \n \nPage 6 of 18 \n \nI mean, while the West Asia conflict is going on one hand, when we look at the \nconsumer momentum on the ground, it's very likely we'll cross 5 million-plus loans \nin the month. Our last peak was 4.6 -odd million loans, and that was in festival \nperiod. So clearly, the company continues to generate momentum. \n Overall, last year, we booked 52.5 million loans and added 17.5 million new \ncustomers on a full year basis. AUM, I talked about grew 22%. PAT grew 24%, ROA , \nand opex to NTI I talked about it, showed an improvement of 36 basis points despite \nrapid expansion of gold loan branches, reasonable expansion of MFI branches by \nthe firm last year.  \nSo that's really on the full year performance. You guys anyway track it, so I don't \nhave to cover that. Let me cover what is important as to how do we see the outlook \nto be. Just on the previous year gone by , the Board of Directors have \nrecommended a dividend to shareholders of INR 6 per equity share.  \nSo that's a 600% dividend. It's broadly in line with the dividend last year as we had \noutlined that part of the dilution that we do in BHFL will be shared with the \nshareholders. So, we've in that spirit, continue to stay on course of that as well. So \nthat's really last year. Let me now go to important panel as to how do we see the \nmanagement assessment to be from a next fiscal standpoint.  \nAs I said, the year so far seems to have started pretty well. We are well on course \nto cross 5 million loans in a single month as a firm. I'm on Panel 28. Of course, I must \njust flag that the FY27 assessment is based on expectations of easing geopolitical \ntensions and macro stability. That  -- I'm sure , it's understood, but I thought it's \nimportant I make the point yet again that it's contingent upon easing geopolitical \ntensions and macro stability. \n Subject to that, the first point is on transformation making the business AI ready. I \nhave already talked about it. We will see significant acceleration and deployment \nof AI use cases across revenue, cost, customer engagement, underwriting, \ncontrollership, security as a firm. In terms of customer franchise, we continue to \nbe confident of adding 15 - 17 million new customers in FY27.  \nIn terms of AUM, we're confident of a 20% to 24% AUM growth, aided by new \nbusinesses that we've launched in the last few years as they begin to scale. Even \nlast year, they contributed 3% of the total growth. Rather 3.5% of total growth even \nin the previous fiscal. And as we gain greater confidence, our market share in those \nbusinesses and their contribution to the business should grow.  \nNIM, we expect marginal moderation. It's a little bit contingent on how interest \nrates play out, caused by geopolitical tensions. On opex to NTI, we expect to see \ncontinued improvement of 25 to 40 basis points from current levels. On credit cost, ", "7": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  April 29, 2026 \n \nPage 7 of 18 \n \nthis is a new credit cost metric, which, as I said we just reported a 165 basis points \nor 1.65% credit cost to AUM, the revised metric.  \nWe expect this to continue to trend down. The reasons I talked about earlier, which \nis winding down of 2-wheeler portfolio and in the MSME business being out of the \nwoods by June'26. In terms of profitability, we are pretty optimistic about the profit \ngrowth. ROA came in as of fourth quarter at 4.6%. We continue to guide for 4.4% to \n4.6%. \n Return on equity came in at 20% in the current quarter, we continue to guide for \n19% to 20%. And GNPA and NNPA, we expect them to remain range bound and well \nwithin our long-term guidance. I think that's really all I have to cover. Just quickly \non BHFL, company has already reported its performance.  \nThey had a good quarter on disbursements, AUM, asset quality, opex and profit, \ntheir disbursements and AUM growth remained strong at 23%. Their loan approvals \nare up a record 37%, opex to NTI continued to improve for them, came in at 19.2% \nas against 21.8% . I'm sure Atul has shared what his long -term view on where he \nforesees opex to NTI to get to, to be a pre -eminent mortgage company in this \ncountry. \n PBT for them in Q4 grew by 20% and PAT excluding onetime reversals, grew by \n20% and asset quality continue to remain very, very healthy with GNPA and NNPA \nat 27 and 11 basis point s respectively. BFSL though small, continue s to grow, \ndelivered a 77% growth in AUM and their profits were up 50%.  \nWe added 124,000 new customers in Q4 and delivered an ROE of 10.5%. So that's \nreally on BFL Q4 and its subsidiaries, the management guidance for FY27. Just the \nlast point, which I did not cover, I should have covered in terms of portfolio quality \nacross Panel 70 onwards,  continue to all be in green are seeing sequential \nimprovements across all portfolios.  \nOnly business loan and professional loans is yellow, which I've articulated twice \nover the last 20 minutes on how we foresee that this should turn. This will not turn \ngreen in a quarter because the denominator also has a role to play as it starts to \ngrow by Q2 or Q3, we will start to see this turn to green. That's really the quarter \ngone by, year gone by and our guidance or management assessment for next year. \nWe are happy to take questions with management. \nModerator: Thank you very much. We will now begin with the question and answer session. The \nfirst question is from the line of Abhishek Murarka from HSBC. Please go ahead. \nAbhishek Murarka: Hi, Rajeev, hi Sandeep. Thanks for taking my question. So just to get this whole \nguidance of credit costs very clear, if you don't mind, can I refer to the press release \nwhere you've given a summary of consolidated financial results? "}, "T2": {"4": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  July 30, 2026 \n \nPage 3 of 22 \n \n Let's jump to panel number five. Excellent quarter, I would say. I mean, I've \nused this word after a long time, probably six  - seven quarters, across all \nmetrics actually, volume momentum, new customer addition. We added 5 \nmillion new customers. AUM, we added a record INR37,000 crores of AUM \nin a quarter. Profit growth was strong at 28% and ROE crossed 20% despite \nthe overhang still there on account of BHFL dilution. S o, a pretty good \nquarter, I would say. ROA came in at 4.7% and ROE at 20.4%. Opex to NTI \ncame in at 33.4%, marginally higher than last year. It will smoothen out as \nI cover over the next few panels. \n Panel six, seven and eight is what I'll quickly cover the key high points. AUM \ngrowth was secular and broad-based, as you can see from the composition \nsummary later in the deck. Gold loan business sustained its strong \nmomentum, grew by 112%. Business is now 4% of AUM. By year -end we \nforesee 2,700 to 2,800 branches, all goes well, maybe 3,000 branches and \nAUM of between INR29,000 crores and INR31,000 crores, all organic. \n Rural consumer finance and urban consumer finance grew by 49% and \n38% due to organic volume growth on one hand, but also increase in SKU \nprices. So out of, let's say, 40% average growth, 20% is on account of SKU \nprice increase and 20% is organic growth. We  foresee further risk in terms \nof SKU prices going up is what the indication that we are principally getting \nfrom OEMs, let's see.  MSME grew by 2%. As you're aware, since last July, \nwe've been pruning business due to sort of risk actions that we've taken. \nIt should come back into growth momentum by Q3 or so. \n NIMs remained steady in Q1. Deposit book came in at INR68,500 crores. It's \nnow 15% of the balance sheet. Opex to NTI came in at 33.4%. The marginal \nincrease principally reflects our accelerated investments in gold loan and \nMFI branch expansion as well as impact of new labor code. So new labor \ncode contributed to 1 0 basis points, INR60 crores. Since you count \ndecimals, I'm making the point on decimals. So, labor code, gold loan and \nMFI branches. Overall, we are confident of delivering 25 to 40 basis points \nimprovement for FY '27 in opex to NTI. Fulltime employee head count stood \nat 73,261. We added 1,650 employees. Excluding gold loan and MFI, the net \naddition was 811. \n On credit quality now, credit performance principally remained quite \nstrong with loan loss to average AUF in the new frame that we talked about ", "6": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  July 30, 2026 \n \nPage 5 of 22 \n \n We're doubling down on Fin AI transformation. We're expanding the AI unit \nfrom 230 to 400 people. We're also adding another 300 people in digital \nplatform unit. It will principally augment our customer centricity, rapidly \naccelerate our technology transfor mation and strengthen our lowest risk \nframework. I mean this year the digital platform will deliver INR50,000 \ncrores of business. \n I am targeting that next year we should be delivering  INR100,000 crores \nvolume between customer centricity, AI and tech transformation and \ndigital transformation, all these three as they come together, that's really \nwhat we are aiming to do so that from a push business, we rapidly \ntransition towards being a full business. In Q1, on Fin AI, 27 bots are now \nlive. But as we get deeper and deeper into this, we find more and more \nopportunities. So it's a continuous transformation frame. \n On data for AI, 45 million customer interactions were analyzed  in the \nquarter through voice and text AI initiatives, and it resulted in 4 lakh \nadditional offers and delivered INR517 crores of disbursements just as a \nresult of better insight into the consumer.  On product and service \ndiscovery, banners, videos, podcasts are enabling faster and more \nefficient marketing. You will see massive acceleration in this from \nNovember, December. massive both from a GEO, SEO and otherwise. \n So, in underwriting processes, we are seeing 20% plus efficiency using AI. \nOn disbursements, AI, voice and text bots did INR2,500 crores, we will do \nINR11,000 crores INR12,000 in the full year this year. On service, AI bots are \nnow handling 71% of our DIY customer service volumes. 17 agentic \napplications have now got deployed out of 118, and we'll rapidly accelerate \nas we get into the year. \n I'm on panel 13. I'll just cover two odd points that it gives you texture on \nour data intelligence, consumer, agent ic AI and custom AI models. The \ninteresting thing is custom AI model, which is on track to go live in Q2. That \nshould help us for our B2B business generate 0.5 million customers that \nwe could not do earlier. It will go live for the next business which will be our \nB2C business that will go live by October, November. So we are beginning \nto have confidence to use  to build models using AI, which are faster, \nnumber one, and gives us insight that our traditional models did not give \nus. That's on AI, I would say. "}, "T1": {"4": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  October 19, 2026 \n \nPage 3 of 19 \n \n MSME grew by 3%. It should now start to grow actually. So , we should start to see \nas we get in the second half, a double -digit kind of growth momentum in the \nbusiness. Gold loan book portfolio continued to grow, it's now 4.1% of balance \nsheet. It was just a tad above INR24,000 crores as of Q2. \n We added 320  odd gold loan branches during the quarter. Total network is now \nacross 2,000 branches. It's at 2 ,022 branches, and we remain  quite clear about \nending the year with close to 2,800 on stand-alone gold branches. \n The rest of the business, as you can see, grew in the corridor of 20% to 30%. So , \noverall pretty secular growth across all lines of business, in a way, if I look at the \ngross loan growth because capital book is dialing down, you should add another \nINR1,000-odd crores that's virtually INR39,000 crores of book growth that we are \nprincipally talking about. These are fascinating numbers even for me, I would say. \nI didn't imagine we'll be growing quarterly between INR38,000 crore s and \nINR39,000 crores on a run rate basis because that's been the case for the last two \nquarters now. \nI'll go back to panel 5, 6 and 7 to quickly give you highlights of the quarter. New \nloans grew 11% to 13.5 million. There is some level of noise in year-on-year number \nbecause last year's season was earlier this year\u2019s season is later.  We are well on \ntrack to deliver between 60 million to 62 million new loans in FY27. We added, as I \nsaid, 4.42 million new customers during the quarter. We are clear about adding \nbetween 18 MM to 20 MM customers to the franchise in FY27. \nOverall franchise I talked about, stood at 129 million and Cross-Sell franchise stood \nat 82 million. Liquidity buffer remained strong at just a tad below 20,000 crores. \nCost of funds came down sequentially by 9 basis points and came in at 7.31%. \nDeposit book is 14%. We've increased rates in Q2. And hopefully, we will see some \nmomentum here as we move forward. Opex to total income improved to 33%. And \ndespite continued investments that we are making in gold loan branches, MFI \nbranches and technology transformation. \nWe remain pretty comfortable place to deliver a 25 to 40 basis point improvement \nin FY27 on opex-to-NTI. Now on credit quality, loan loss to average AUF improved \nto 1.56% from 1.88% last year. As a measure of prudence, we recognized, as I said, \na macroeconomic overlay of 337 crores. Excluding this provision, the loan losses \nand provisions actually declined year-on-year by 12% and loan loss to average AUF \nin the process would look to be like 1.32%. \nYou may see here that the Stage 2 and Stage 3 assets actually increased by 1,601 \ncrores. It actually includes a 1,047 crores increase in Stage 2 in the gold loan \nbusiness, due to the short tenure schemes that we have actually launched when \nthe new RBI guidelines came out and that has had an impact. We have since August \nstopped doing short-tenor schemes as a firm. We don't see any credit cost impact ", "5": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  October 19, 2026 \n \nPage 4 of 19 \n \nas a result of it already out of the 1,099 crores, which is close to 1,100 crores came \nfrom gold loan in Stage 2, 550 crores has already been resolved and has flown back \nto Stage 1. So, it's a transient movement for the quarter is really what we see it to \nbe and don't see any impact on credit costs or even otherwise or in terms of auction \nand so on and so forth. \nVintage credit performance across our 3MOB, 6MOB, 9MOB continues to reflect \nsignificant and sustained improvement in the portfolio. And we are reasonably \ncomfortable with the credit cost outlook for FY27. Of course, we remain in a VUCA \nworld, so we'll take quarterly derived. GNPA and NNPA of course, if first quarter -- \nfirst half is to go by, we remain pretty comfortably placed on credit quality at this \npoint in time for FY27. PCR remained pretty strong at 60%. GNPA and NNPA came \nin at 1.06 and 42 basis points. ROE, ROA I talked about 4.6% and 20.6%. \nLet me just cover now on capital raised because that's something that we \nannounced recently on October 1 as you're aware the Board approved subject to \nshareholder approval. 11,700 crores of QIP and preferential warrants by Bajaj \nFinserv to the tune of 5,80 0 crores in accordance of course, with subject to \napprovals by the shareholders. \nThe Board's decision of raising additional capital is principally driven by mainly \ngrowth story of India and of the company and its subsidiaries. That's the principal \nreason why we're raising capital. And we are very also clear that without having to \ndilute in any given manner, the sustainability of the medium-term goals of ROA and \nROE that we have actually outlined to shareholders. \nIt's pretty much for growth that we are looking to raise capital as we move forward. \nQuickly, let me now cover panel 9 to 11. I won't cover too much, but we continue to \ndouble down on FINAI transformation. We now have scaled our AI unit to 328 odd \npeople. Given the breadth and the depth of the work that we're doing, the facial \nrecognition camera technology is now deployed in close to 534 stores and 1 ,163 \nservice branches. We're getting a lot of learnings from these deployments. AI bots \nare now handling 75% of our DIY customer service volumes, disbursements, you \ncan see the numbers, year-on-year 111%. \nSo, as we deepen our understanding of enterprise AI and deploy it, we get \nexperience, we get learnings. We're becoming more and more effective, both in \nterms of cost and in terms of experience of the customer and of the employee. \nOverall company has no 1 30-odd agents and 65 agentic applications. We are well \non track to deliver 180-odd agentic applications and 600-odd agents for the year. \nSo, I won\u2019t cover Panel 13 and 14, just the only point I'll cover on Panel 13 - 14 is that \nwe\u2019ve launched our first customer risk model from a risk management standpoint, \nwe'll book 500,000 accounts in our B2B business, look at its 3MOB performance. ", "12": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  October 19, 2026 \n \nPage 11 of 19 \n \nKuntal Shah: Thanks. This is good conservative stance and thanks for this clarification. I will join \nthe queue. \nModerator: Thank you very much. Next question is from the line of Kunal Shah from Citi. Please \ngo ahead. \nKunal Shah: Yes. Hi, so the first question is on growth guidance. Last time you said like you \nreview post the second quarter, you said it's very fascinating to see almost 26% \ngrowth plus the capital raise is also for the growth story. So, what would be the \ngrowth guidance here onwards for near and medium term? So that's the first \nquestion? \nAnd second, in terms of the overall margins, cost of funds are down 9 basis points, \nbut given that we are in the rate hike scenario, we had still seen some margin \ncompression and NIMs on calculated basis appear to be down. So how do we see \nonce cost of funds start to see up? What is the outlook on margins, yes? \nRajeev Jain: So, I would say FY27, two quarters gone. We're seeing the early onset of festival \nfirst 9, 10 days. I would say probably between 26%, 27% growth we should deliver \nnow on a full year basis. I think that's just medium term, we maintain a guidance of \n20% to 25% on a year lower here and a year higher there.  \nSo that's the second point. On NIM, clearly, at this point in time, we are baking in \ntwo more increases in the current year that would take us to 6% kind of rates. You \nwould see marginal moderation on NIM. As you move from here, we should be able \nto manage comfortably is what I would say in terms of NIM because part of that \nwould come from opex to NTI. Part of th is would come in from, of course, some \ncredit costs as you're seeing. So overall profitability and return ratio, we should be \nable to continue to navigate is what I would say to you. \nKunal Shah: But moderation in NIM would be only marginal. That's what we are suggesting? \nRajeev Jain: Yes, at this point in time, yes. Answer is yes. You've seen, I think 10 - 12 basis points \ncompression in Q1 to Q2. \nSandeep Jain: Yes. I think H1 NIM over March is about 5 basis points lower. The Q1 was marginally \nhigher. Q2 was 10- 12 basis points lower than Q1. So, we are seeing moderation in \nNIM. It remains manageable.  \nKunal Shah: From a Q2 level, not much of an impact on margins? \nSandeep Jain: I'm not saying that. That's what you are saying. I think we continue to remain in \ncost of fund increased environment. We do mindful of it. In the last quarter, we \nhave done a significant amount of interest rate hedging that has brought down the \ncost of fund effectively in the last quarter at BFL and BHFL level.  ", "15": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  October 19, 2026 \n \nPage 14 of 19 \n \nModerator: Next question is from Avinash Singh from Emkay Global. \nAvinash Singh: Good evening. So of course, on capital ra ise, you have outlined the growth at the \nunderlying thing. But if I look, I mean, your profitability, also the possible stake sale \nfrom our subsidiary Bajaj Housing. And also, the timing in the past at your level \nwhen you speculate thing I mean, it looks, I would say, much of in advance or to \nprudent. So exactly, what kind of a growth, because you have too many questions, \nincluding your current Tier 1, so that is such a large capital raise plan at this earlier \nstage? \n And second, on insurance thing, if at all, this regulation assuming it is were to come \nthrough, will you remain an open architecture or there will be some kind of a \nrethinking distributing your own group's product or irrespective of commission \nregulation, you remain an open architecture? Thanks. \nRajeev Jain: Yes. So I'll take the second one first. We'll wait for the final regulations to come to \nmake a decision, is what I would just say. It's a discussion paper. It has to get \nconverted into draft regulations and then regulations. No point in speculating at \nthis point in time is what I would just say to you. \n As I said earlier, we are creating multiple scenarios for us as a firm as to how will \nwe navigate while protecting our fees and margin. So that's the only point I would \nmake. We are clear about the medium -term guidance on fees and margins. So \nthat's point number one. \n Point number two, your question is principally on capital. As I said, it's principally \nfor growth. As I said, we added close to INR38,500 crores in the quarter itself, 26.5% \ngrowth. I'm not changing, I must say to you, the longer -term growth guidance of \n23% to 25%. \n I think it's important to make that point clear because that's a good number to \ncompound at the levels we are in. In a year, we may grow 27%, in a year, we may \ngrow 23%. But long-term compounding is really what we are principally focused on \ncoming from und erlying quality of the business and the margin profile of the \nbusiness, so just at a philosophical level, to make the point. \n The third point, which is important, which I would like to highlight, and we've been \ncovering that in our Investor Day for a long, long time, actually. And I'll break this \ninto two parts, because as you said, we could use it to dilute or do dilution in BHFL \nrather than raising capital. \n One, last 3 years ago, we raised INR10,000 crores Since then, exactly the balance \nsheet ending this year would have grown double. So in 3 years, we've actually \ndoubled. So I should be rising 20. I'm raising or looking to 17.5, so I think that's point \nnumber one. So in absolute, the number may look large, but in terms of previous ", "3": "  Bajaj Finance Limited \n  October 19, 2026 \n \nPage 2 of 19 \n \n  \n \nModerator: Ladies and gentlemen, good day, and welcome to Bajaj Finance Q2 FY27 Earnings \nConference Call. As a reminder, all participant lines will be in the listen-only mode, \nand there will be an opportunity for you to ask questions after the presentation \nconcludes. Should you need assistance during this conference call, please signal \nan operator by pressing star then zero on your touchtone phone.  \n I'm pleased to welcome Mr. Rajeev Jain, Vice Chairman and Managing Director; and \nMr. Sandeep Jain, COO and CFO; and other senior members of the management \nteam. I now invite Mr. Rajeev Jain for his opening remarks, post which we will open \nthe floor for the Q&A session. Thank you, and over to you, Mr. Jain. \nRajeev Jain: Thank yo u for hosting us . Very good evening to all of you, or good morning, \ndepending on the geography you're in. Welcome to the BFL Q2 earnings call. The \ninvestor deck for Q2 has been uploaded on our website. I hope you had a chance to \ngo through the same. I'll quickly focus on key updates for the quarter and our \nmanagement assessment before Q&A. I expect to between 15 to 20 odd minutes. \n Let's quickly jump to Panel 4 in terms of the quarter went by. Overall, I would say it \nis an excellent quarter with strong performance across all key metrics, of AUM, new \ncustomer addition, credit cost, profit growth, ROA and ROE. AUM, as you can see, \ngrew by 26.5% to INR 5,84,624 crores, delivering net AUM addition of close to INR \n38,000 crores in the quarter. PBT grew by 29% to INR 8,537 crores in Q2. This is \nbefore the macroeconomic provision that we've created of another INR 337 crores \nto grew by 34% to INR 8,874 crores. \n PAT grew by 29% to INR6,367 crores. Before macro  overlay provision, it grew by \n34% to INR6,619 crores. In Q2, company booked just a tad below INR13.5 million \nnew loans and added 4.5 million new customers. Overall customer franchise stood \nat just a tad below 129 million customers. \n ROA for the quarter came in at 4.6% and ROE came in at 20.6%. Opex to total income \nwas 33% and net NPA came in at 42 basis points. Overall, I would say, a pretty good \nquarter for the firm and first half for the firm. \n In general, AUM growth was pretty secular. I'll quickly go through -- I've jumped \nactually to Panel 52. If you see, the growth is pretty secular, urban consumer \nfinance grew by 43%, rural consumer finance by 63%. MFI grew on a low base, grew \n54%, CV and Tractor on low base grew 89%, LAS grew 42%, captive book, as you're \naware, as I've been talking for the last 2 quarters, continues to wind down and \nwould create significant tailwinds for us in terms of credit costs as you move \nforward is now down to just INR2,400-odd crores. "}}}

